In the field of materials engineering of luminescent centres the grand challenges encompass:
- Increased brightness (target: at least \(10^{2}\) cd \(\mathrm{m}^{-2}\)) and emission quantum yield (\(>0.50\), for excitation in the deep UV/blue), enhanced absorption cross-section (5–6 orders of magnitude larger than upon intra-4f excitation) and broadened excitation range (\(>100\ \mathrm{nm}\));
- More environmentally-friendly phosphors allowing excitation by cheap, safe and cold LEDs;
- Excellent photostability under UV/blue irradiation, for organic and hybrid materials (no degradation for \(>20\ \mathrm{h}\) at long UV/blue irradiation);
- New electroluminescent materials (at least \(10^{2}\) cd \(\mathrm{m}^{-2}\), for an applied voltage \(<10\ \mathrm{V}\));
- Multifunctional systems, combining light emission with other properties, including porosity.
This critical review attempts to show that a particular class of materials known as metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) containing \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions (LnMOFs) constitutes a robust platform for addressing these challenges. In particular, we wish to highlight, and urge the reader to explore, the multifunctional nature of these systems, which may combine light emission with several other properties, such as microporosity, magnetism, chirality, molecule and ion sensing, catalysis and activity as multimodal imaging contrast agents. LnMOFs present a unique chance of observing synergistic effects such as the coupling between photoluminescence and magnetism (e.g. modulating photoluminescence with a magnetic field). The above list of challenges also sets the scene for chemists interested in LnMOFs and assists in defining targets and directing future research.
MOFs and related coordination polymers are crystalline organic–inorganic hybrid materials with frameworks built by linking polyatomic metal clusters entirely by strong covalent interactions.\(^{4,5}\) Luminescent MOFs are still at an early stage of development. Although well over 200 papers have been
published, most reports are limited to the presentation of luminescence spectra and detailed spectroscopic analyses (excitation spectra, lifetimes, emission quantum yields or quenching rate constants) are still rare. The combination of organic and inorganic components and nanoporosity create a wealth of possibilities available in few other classes of materials. Both the metal and the linker can be used to give rise to luminescence, and can furthermore interact (via antenna effect) to increase the brightness and the quantum yield. As a result, chemical sensing is clearly a viable application. Moreover, the high surface areas available in some MOFs suggest the potential to create hybrid materials that serve both as preconcentrator and detection medium.
Allendorf et al. have recently published an excellent overview of the field of luminescent MOFs and the reader is referred to their work.\(^{6}\) Therefore, we will not review a number of aspects of the photoluminescence of LnMOFs, including general concepts, and will concentrate on exploring the remarkable multifunctionality of these materials, which allows a novel approach to meet the grand challenges of materials engineering of luminescent centres. We consider mainly papers published in the last 4–5 years.
## 2. Nanoparticles of LnMOFs
MOFs on the nanometre scale can offer an interesting approach to the design of functional nanomaterials for biological and biomedical applications, mainly because their compositions can be systematically tuned by a judicious choice of the building blocks.\(^{7}\) Moreover, the use of LnMOFs in devices and other practical applications, and the preparation of nanocomposites (including those consisting of luminophores bound to the MOF surface), will often require the use of nanoparticles. Unfortunately, work on nanoparticles of LnMOFs (and, to a lesser extent, on MOFs, NMOFs) is scanty and lacks, in most cases, generality. Clearly, much more research is needed in this area.
