## 3. Near-infrared (NIR) emitting LnMOFs
The increase in optical communications traffic is stimulating much research into the development of highly-efficient broadband-fibre amplifiers in the wide-window transmission of silica-based optical fibres \((1300{-}1700~\mathrm{~nm})\) . \({\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\) -based emitting materials in this range may have a tremendous impact in the new generation of optical amplifiers for wavelength division multiplexing network systems, in the second and third telecommunication windows, around 1.3 and \(1.5\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) , respectively, where the loss in silica-based fibres is low and the chromatic dispersion of the fibres is weak.
The research on luminescent LnMOFs has been focused on their visible emission features, whereas emissive NIR LnMOFs (usually \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\,=\,\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}\) , \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}\) ) are barely studied, mainly because: (i) most ligands are poor sensitisers of \({\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}\) , \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}\) , with molar absorption coefficients typically lower than \(10\;\mathrm{Lmol}^{-1}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ,2 and (ii) the energy gap between the first excited emitting state and the fundamental level is quite small (ca. 5400 and ca. \(6500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}\) , respectively) and easily matched by the \(\mathrm{C{-}H}\) , \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{C}\) , \(\mathrm{O-H}\) , and \(\mathrm{N-H}\) vibrations of the organic framework, which
provide suitable non-radiative channels. Through careful structural design it is, however, possible to overcome such limitations.
Herrera et al. explored a large family of materials (one-dimensional helical chains or ladders, two-dimensional sheets or three-dimensional frameworks) having \(\mathrm{Ru-CN-Ln}\) bridges \((\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\,=\,\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}\) , \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}\) ), in which the \({\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\) NIR luminescence could be efficiently sensitised due to a \(\mathrm{Ru}\,\rightarrow\,\mathrm{Ln}\) energy transfer (with a rate larger than \(10^{8}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}\) ).13 The observed very short \(^2\mathrm{F}_{5/2}\) \((\mathrm{Yb}^{3+})\) , \(^4\mathrm{F}_{3/2}\) \((\mathrm{Nd}^{3+})\) and \(^4\mathrm{I}_{13/2}\) \((\mathrm{Er}^{3+})\) lifetimes (one order of magnitude smaller than those of molecular complexes, which are typically 10, 1 and \(2~\upmu\mathrm{s}\) , for \({\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}\) , \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}\) , respectively) are explained by the presence of the cross-linked cyanide-bridged network, which seems to provide a route for the rapid dispersal of phonons through the lattice.13
In addition, White et al. discussed an interesting example in which \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pvdc}\) was used to efficiently sensitise the NIR emission of the \({\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}\) ions at ca. \(1000\,\mathrm{\nm}\) in an extended \([\mathrm{Yb}_{2}(\mathrm{pvdc})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}6\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}8.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) porous network.14 When co-doped with multiple \({\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\) ions the LnMOFs can be used as bimodal (or multicoloured) light emitters. Bimodal (or multicoloured) emission may be applied in multiplexed detection and imaging of therapeutic cells, in vitro and in vivo (when a specific fluorescence image can be selected using appropriate optical filters).15 A very innovative application was reported by White et al. that proposed a new approach for creating luminescent barcoded systems based on MOF materials containing multiple NIR emitting \({\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\) ions (Fig. 1).16 They described \(\mathrm{[(Er_{x}Y b_{1-x})_{2}(p v d c)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot}6D M F{\cdot}8.5H_{2}O}\) (with \(x\,=\,0.32\) , 0.58, 0.70 and 0.81) materials with different amounts of \({\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}\) ions that were excited by the same chromophore ligand. The relative intensity of the \({\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}\) NIR emissions are linearly correlated to their ratio and by controlling the reactant stoichiometry. The resulting lanthanide composition and the concomitant \({\mathrm{Yb}}^{3+}/{\mathrm{Er}}^{3+}\) barcode NIR fingerprint are fine tuned. The number and diversity of barcodes may, thus, be increased by using a large number of \(\mathrm{Ln}/\mathrm{Ln}\) ratios or by incorporating additional lanthanide cations into the material. The latter idea was demonstrated with \(\mathrm{[(Nd_{0.09}E r_{0.55}Y b_{0.36})_{2}(p v d c)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot}6D M F{\cdot}8.5H_{2}O}\) (Fig. 1b).
