## 4. Up-conversion in LnMOFs
Up-conversion is a non-linear optical process in which a sequential absorption of two or more pump photons originates light emission with higher energy than the pump source, via intermediate long-lived excited states (Fig. 2). UC emission from \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) compounds offers an attractive optical labelling technique in biological studies without many of the restrictions associated with organic chromophores and quantum dots. The use of NIR, instead of UV, photoexcitation minimises background autofluorescence, photobleaching, and photodamage to biological specimens. The technique also
allows in vivo observation with high spatial resolution and large sample penetration depths (much higher than those obtained by UV excitation). In addition, UC processes can be induced by low power \((1{-}10^{3}\ \mathrm{W\cm}^{-2})\) continuous wave lasers, as opposed to a costly high-intensity \((10^{6}–10^{9}\;\mathrm{W}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) pulse laser source for the generation of a simultaneous twophoton process.25 Furthermore, as high-power NIR pump lasers are easily available, efficient NIR-to-visible UC may play an important role in display technologies. To date, most of the reported UC \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) -based materials concentrate on cooperative \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions embedded in host lattices, as pioneered by Auzel.26 The use of LnMOFs as UC materials is rarely reported due to the existence of effective multiphonon relaxation which decreases the efficiency of the UC process.27,28
Jin et al. reported blue, green and red UC emissions in \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}/\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}\) co-doped \([(Y:\mathrm{Er},\mathrm{Yb})(\mathrm{oba})(\mathrm{ox})_{0.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{2}^{28}\) and \([(Y:\mathrm{Er},\mathrm{Yb})_{3}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3.5}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) .29 While the green \((^{2}\mathrm{H}_{11/2},\,^{2}\mathrm{S}_{3/2}\,\rightarrow\,^{4}\mathrm{I}_{15/2}\) , at ca. 520 and \(540\,\mathrm{\nm}\) , respectively) and red \((^{4}\mathrm{F}_{9/2}\rightarrow{^4}\mathrm{I}_{15/2}\) at ca. \(660\;\mathrm{nm}\) ) emissions were explained by the conventional two-photon mechanism, a three-photon process was proposed to explain the blue components at ca. \(407\,\mathrm{\nm}\) \((^{2}\mathrm{H}_{9/2}\,\rightarrow\,^{4}\mathrm{I}_{15/2})\) in \([(Y:\mathrm{Er},\mathrm{Yb})(\mathrm{oba})(\mathrm{ox})_{0.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{2}^{28}\) and at ca. \(455\;\mathrm{nm}\) \((^{4}\mathrm{F}_{5/2}\rightarrow{^4}\mathrm{I}_{15/2})\) in \([(Y:\mathrm{Er},\mathrm{Yb})(\mathrm{bdc})_{3.5}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (Fig. 2).29 Green and red UC emissions, explained by the conventional two-photon mechanism, were also reported in \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}/\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}\) co-doped \([(Y:\mathrm{Er},\mathrm{Yb})(\mathrm{pza})(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{\cdot}^{29}\)
Interesting examples of visible-to-visible UC upon pulselaser excitation at \(580\ \mathrm{nm}\) are reported by Yang et al.30 and Mahata et al.31 for \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) -MOFs. In the former, the distance between the dinuclear \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) ions (bridged by the carboxylate groups) in \([\mathrm{Nd}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{3}(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}]{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) is \(4.10\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , which enables facile energy transfer between two optically active \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) centres, resulting in an efficient UC blue emission through energy transfer. Energy-transfer UC involves two ions in close proximity that were excited by ground-state absorption to their respective metastable intermediate states. The emitting state may also be reached by a sequence of ground- and excited-states absorption steps.30 In \([\mathrm{Nd}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{pda})(\mathrm{Hbdc})_{2}]\) UC blue emission ascribed to the \(^{4}\mathrm{D}_{3/2}\,\rightarrow\,^{4}\mathrm{I}_{11/2,13/2,15/2}\,\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) transitions was achieved by a sequence of ground-state absorption to the \(^4\mathrm{G}_{5/2}\) level, followed by a rapid nonradiative relaxation to the \(^4\mathrm{F}_{3/2}\) level, and an excited-state absorption from this level to the \(^4\mathrm{D}_{5/2}\) state which also relax non-radiatively to the \(^4\mathrm{D}_{3/2}\) level emitting one photon (Fig. 3a). The number of photons involved in the UC process was determined by plotting on a log–log scale the luminescence intensity vs. the excitation intensity (Fig. 3b). The three main emitting regions (364, 422 and \(431\ \mathrm{nm}\) ) showed a slope larger than one, indicating that the observed UC blue emission is a two-photon process.27,31
## 5. Porous LnMOFs as sensors
Some work is available on assessing the potential of LnMOFs as sensor materials, although actual sensing devices are still missing. The development of these sensors often requires some degree of porosity. Coupling \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) luminescence and porosity is, in itself, challenging, particularly because the high and variable coordination numbers of \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) centres often promote
the formation of dense solids. Moreover, the as-prepared porous structures usually do not survive the removal of solvent molecules. A third limitation lies in the fact that only a few ligands are able to form porous architectures and act, at the same time, as efficient antennae to sensitise \({\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\) . Chendler et al. highlighted these difficulties and reported a series of isostructural mixed-metal porous solids formulated as \([\mathrm{Ba}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}][\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{dbd})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},\) , where \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\,=\,\mathrm{Sm}^{3+}\) , \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) , \({\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}\) , \({\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}\) , \(\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}\) ,32 \(\mathrm{dbd}^{2-}\) was found to be a good antenna for all \({\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\) , with the exception of \({\mathrm{Gd}}^{3+}\) . To circumvent the formation of dense, non-porous solids, they used a stepwise or Aufbau approach: firstly, \({\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\) was reacted with \(\mathrm{dbd}^{2-}\) in a \(3:1\) ratio to obtain a metalloligand (metal complex) and, subsequently, these units were cross-linked into a solid network by adding \(\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}\) to form the final MOF. In a continuation of this work, the same authors reacted \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{dbd}\) with the same \({\mathrm{Ln}}^{3+}\) ions, to obtain a series of photoluminescent porous solids formulated as \([\mathrm{Na}_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}][\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{dbd})_{4}]\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) .33
Several examples of molecular recognition and sensing of small molecules, cations and anions by MOFs have been reported and reviewed by Chen et al.34 We now present some
recent work on photoluminescence-based sensing of chemical species by LnMOFs.
## 5.1 Cation sensing
Lu et al. have shown that exchanging the 'zeolitic' \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) ions of \(\mathrm{K}_{5}[\mathrm{Tb}_{5}(\mathrm{idc})_{4}(\mathrm{ox})_{4}]\) by \(\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\) increases the luminescence emission considerably.35 Other cations (e.g. \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}\) ) do not change the luminescence or are able to quench it (e.g. \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) ). Luo and Batten also explored the ion-exchange route but, instead, they started out from a non-LnMOF, \([\mathrm{NH}_{4}]_{2}[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{btc})]\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , which they doped with \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) or \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) through ion exchange of \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\) .36 These doped materials were then ion-exchanged with \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) , \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) and a range of transition metals. Certain metals (e.g. alkali metals) do not quench the luminescence emission while others, such as \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) (in the case of \(\mathrm{Cu-Eu}@\mathrm{MOF}\) ) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) (Co–Tb@MOF), are
efficient quenchers and this effect may be used for the detection of aqueous \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\).
A clever way of sensing metal ions relies on the immobilisation of Lewis basic centres within porous MOFs. These types of MOFs are, however, difficult to prepare because such Lewis basic sites tend to bind other metal ions to form condensed structures. \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions, however, show preferential binding to carboxylate oxygen atoms over pyridyl nitrogen atoms in \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\)-pyridinecarboxylate complexes. Exploiting this fact, Chen et al. prepared \(\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{pdc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{DMF})\mathrm{J}0.5\mathrm{DMF}0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), with Lewis basic pyridyl sites for the sensing of metal ions. The material is porous and the basic Lewis sites line the interior of the channels, which may be emptied of DMF and water molecules by heating at ca. \(200\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The solid was immersed in DMF solutions of different ions. Only ions such as \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), which were found to weakly bind to the pyridyl nitrogen atoms, were able to quench the luminescence emission significantly.
Yet another possible route for recognition of ions by MOFs is suggested by the work of Jiang et al., who have synthesised materials formulated as \([\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{bco})_{2}(\mathrm{bipy})]\) containing both a weakly coordinating ligand (bipy) and a stronger coordinating one \((\mathrm{bco}^{2-})\) which is able to establish robust joints (Fig. 4a); bipy may be leached out under reflux in \(\mathrm{CDCl}_{3}\), generating a hollow oxygen-rich framework \([\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{bco})_{2}]\) (Fig. 4b). Subsequently, chemical species may be introduced by reversible encapsulation. In their paper, Jiang et al. encapsulated \([\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{8}]^{3+}\) and mixed \([\mathrm{Ln},\mathrm{Ln}^{\prime}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{8}]^{3+}\) cations \((\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}=\mathrm{Th}^{3+};\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}=\mathrm{Eu}^{3+},\mathrm{Nd}^{3+})\) and, as a result, the dual- and bimodal emissions were realised from different \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) emission centers. Presumably, other chemical species may be encapsulated and detected. We note in passing that \([\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{bco})_{2}(\mathrm{bipy})]\) is photochromic. Upon exposure to sunlight (ca. 2 days) it undergoes a radical-based single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformation from a colourless material to a purple one. Very few examples of photochromic MOFs have been reported.
So far, the research on porous MOFs for their biological or medicinal applications has been limited to drug delivery, medical gas delivery and MRI contrast agents, all still at an early stage. The following examples by Xiao et al. and Harbuzaru et al. highlight the potential of the emerging
porous LnMOFs as luminescent cation sensors in biological systems. The former work reports a microporous luminescent \([\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{fum})_{2}(\mathrm{ox})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), for highly selective and sensitive sensing of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) in aqueous solution. The efficient, simple and real time detection of trace amounts of \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) in the brain may help in finding clues for understanding and treating Wilson's and Alzheimer's diseases.
A particular case of cation sensing concerns \(\mathrm{H}^{+}\), which has relevant importance in biological systems. Harbuzaru et al. reported the construction of a miniaturised prototype for pH sensing in the pH range of biological interest (5–7.5), based on a photoluminescent \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\)-MOF that contains two different \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) sites (Eu1 and Eu2) and self-assembles through hydrogen bonding and \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking interactions (Fig. 5a). This material (named ITQMOF-3) has a very high quantum yield (0.56 at an excitation wavelength of \(350\,\mathrm{nm}\)) and an excellent balance between absorption, energy transfer and emission rate. The ligand used was a well-known and powerful sensitising ligand for \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PhenDCA}\) (L), in which both carboxylic and phenanthroline moieties may coordinate to the metal center. ITQMOF-3 may be described as a layered
compound with two well-defined and very different sheets, A and B. Each sheet contains one type of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) (Eu1 or Eu2): sheet A, containing Eu1, has an \(\mathrm{EuL}_{2}\) stoichiometry and is negatively charged; sheet B contains \(\mathrm{Eu}^{2}\) with stoichiometry \(\mathrm{[Eu_{2}L(OH)(H_{2}O)_{4}]}\) and is positively charged. In this latter case, the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) centres form dimers connected through an OH group \((\mathrm{Eu-O-Eu};\ d_{\mathrm{Eu-Eu}}\ =\ 4.61\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) The presence of two different \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions is confirmed by the emission spectrum that displays two non-degenerate \(^5\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow\,^{7}\mathrm{F}_{0}\) transition lines at 579.0 (Eu2) and 580.7 (Eu1) nm (Fig. 5b). It turns out that increasing the \(\mathrm{pH}\) of a solution in contact with the MOF crystals enhances the intensity of the Eu2 line while the Eu1 line remains constant. Thus, this sensor requires no calibration, and by following the intensity ratio \(\left(I_{\mathrm{r}}\right)\) of the \(^5\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow\,^{7}\mathrm{F}_{0}\) emissions of the two \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) environments it is possible to determine the \(\mathrm{pH}\) of the solution to be analysed. By taking advantage of the high emission quantum efficiency and \(\mathrm{pH}\) -sensing capability it was possible to design a new miniaturised \(\mathrm{pH}\) sensor prototype by combining the material with a commercial \(1.5\,\mathrm{\mm}\) diameter optic fibre. If suitable nanocrystals of this material could be internalised in cells it would be possible to measure intracellular \(\mathrm{pH}\) , which is a parameter of high biorelevance.
Other recent examples of cation \((\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+})\) sensing by MOFs include the unusual heterometallic frameworks based on \([\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) -high spin \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}]\) and \([\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}\mathrm{-Ag}^{+}]\) -pyridine-2,6- dicarboxylic acid, reported by Zhao et al.41,42
## 5.2 Anion sensing
The sensing of anions by MOFs has been comparatively less studied. However, as Chen et al. point out, given the richness of \(\mathrm{N-H}\) and \(\mathrm{O-H}\) containing linkers and terminal organic solvents within MOFs to induce hydrogen bonding interactions with anions, microporous MOFs are expected to be promising materials for the recognition and sensing of anions.43 These authors reported a luminescent MOF formulated as [Tb(btc)]-G \(\mathrm{G}=\) guest solvent) whose luminescence intensity increases upon incorporation of different amounts of \(\mathrm{NaX}\) \((\mathbf{X}^{-}=\mathbf{F}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) and \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) ) or \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{X}\) \((\mathbf{X}^{2-}\,=\,\mathbf{CO}_{3}^{\,\,2-}\) and \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}{}^{2-}\) ) in methanol. The intensity increase is similar for all anions except \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) for which the intensity enhancement is particularly large. This selectivity for \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) is also observed with other solvents such as DMF. Chen et al. also discussed the possible luminescence enhancement mechanism. In short, they suggested that hydrogen bonding between the anion and solvent methanol, \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\cdot\cdot\mathrm{H-OCH}_{3}\) , modifies the energy of the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) bond stretching, leading to the observed intensity changes. This mechanism for recognition and sensing of anions, relying on \(\mathrm{O-H}\) moieties within terminal solvents, is different from the mechanism reported for \([\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{muc})_{1.5}]\) , in which the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) groups of the organic linker (mucic acid is rich in OH groups) interact strongly with the anions through hydrogen bonding, thus reducing the vibrational movements of the channels and enhancing the emission intensity.44 Indeed, upon addition of an aqueous solution of sodium salts of \(\mathrm{F}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{Br^{-}}\) , \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\) , \(\mathrm{CN^{-}}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{\ 2-}\) to a solid sample of \([\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{muc})_{1.5}]\) , Wong et al. observed an enhancement of the emission. The largest increase was observed for the latter
anion, while no significant enhancement was found for \(\mathrm{SO}_{4}{}^{2-}\) and \(\mathrm{PO}_{4}{}^{3-}\) .44
## 5.3 Molecule sensing
Chen et al. made use of luminescent open (unsaturated) \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) centres within MOFs as the sensing sites for bonding of substrates.45 They illustrated the concept with \(\mathrm{[Eu(btc)(H_{2}O)]\cdot1.5H_{2}O_{:}}\) , a LnMOF which possesses pores too large \((c a.\ 6.6\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}\ \times\ 6.6\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) to perform a sieving function with respect to the studied solvent molecules: acetone, 1-propanol and DMF. The \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) open metal sites were generated by removal of free and terminal water molecules after simple calcination of \([\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{btc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\cdot1.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) at \(140\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . The emission intensity of these 'activated' LnMOF crystals depended largely on the solvents in which they were immersed. DMF and acetone exhibited the most significant enhancing and quenching effects, respectively. Admittedly, although the mechanisms for such effects of small solvent molecules are still unclear, the binding interaction of the luminescent open metal sites with guest molecules probably plays an important role.
In another study with MOF-76, \([\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{btc})]\mathrm{-DMF}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) , the fluorescence intensity of emulsions in \(n\) -propanol increased and decreased significantly with the addition of DMF and acetone solvents, respectively.46 This was attributed to the energy transfer between solvent molecules and the btc3 ligand (antenna) resulting from the absorption properties of the solvent molecules and interaction between solvent and organic ligand.
Harbuzaru et al. reported a new family of nanoporous LnMOFs with \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -(hexafluoroisopropylidene)bis(benzoic acid) (hereafter named ITQMOF-1, Fig. 6a), exhibiting large emission quantum yields (up to 0.48) and the highest \(^5\mathrm{D}_{0}\) efficiencies (up to 0.83) reported for solid-state \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) compounds with organic ligands.47 To show that these materials have potential applications in sensor devices, ITQMOF-1-Eu was used to sense ethanol in air. The \(619\ \mathrm{nm}\) emission was monitored under an air stream alternatively saturated/not saturated with ethanol. Fig. 6b shows a rapid decrease of the emission intensity in the presence of ethanol and a rapid recovery when the sample is exposed to air. This behaviour may be rationalised by considering that ethanol coordinates the \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions, thus quenching the emission through coupling with the vibrational states of the \(\mathrm{O-H}\) oscillators. Similar results were obtained by exposure to a water–ethanol mixture, confirming the interest of the hydrophobic properties of the materials.
Other recent examples of sensing of molecules by MOFs are the reports of Jiang et al.48 and Lin et al.49 Because of its prevalence in many synthesis systems and notorious action as a photoluminescence quencher, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) is a molecule of particular interest in the context of LnMOFs. Shi et al. reported that the dehydration of \(\mathrm{Na}_{4}[\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathrm{hedp})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) induces prominent changes in the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) emission lines, namely in the energy, full-width-at-half-maximum and number of Stark components, which decreases and red-shifts the \(^5\mathrm{D}_{0}\ \rightarrow\ ^{7}\mathrm{F}_{0}\) transition.50 The decrease of the energy of the latter transition indicates that, on average, the Eu–O bonds are slightly less
covalent in the dehydrated material.2 After rehydration, the emission spectra resemble those of the as-synthesised material indicating a reversibility of the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) local environment. From the emission spectra and \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\) lifetime values, it was possible to estimate the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\) quantum efficiency and infer the presence and number of water molecules in the first coordination sphere of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\).
Zu et al. reported the family \(\mathrm{[Ln_{2}(fum)_{2}(ox)(H_{2}O)_{4}]{\cdot}4H_{2}O}\) \((\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}~=~\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) , \({\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}\) ) of porous frameworks. While the as-prepared hydrated materials are crystalline, the dehydrated solids are amorphous. The process is reversible upon rehydration.51 Oddly enough, the materials display a reversible and large change in their luminescence intensities upon de-/rehydration, whereby water effectively enhances (rather than decreases) the luminescence. This unusual reverse role of water was rationalised by considering that the local \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) environment was softened in the amorphous dehydrated phases while it recovered stiffness upon rehydration.
A conceptually very different way of sensing chemical species using LnMOFs is based on the surface modification of nanoparticles of such materials.9 Initially, nanoparticles of a \(5~\mathrm{mol\%}\) Eu-doped \([\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{bdc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\) were coated with poly(vinylpyrrolidone) molecules and then with a silica nanoshell (upon treatment with TEOS). The silica shell was further functionalised with a silylated Tb-EDTA monoamide derivative for the luminescence sensing of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) pydc, which is a major constituent of many pathogenic spore-forming bacteria. Upon excitation at \(278~\mathrm{nm}\), this Tb-EDTA composite only gave
\(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) luminescence because the Tb-EDTA moiety is essentially non-emissive. However, when \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) pydc was added to an ethanolic dispersion of the nanocomposite, the \({\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}\) luminescence became clearly visible due to formation of the Tb-EDTA- \(\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\) pydc complex. The \({\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}\) luminescence signal provides a sensitive probe for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) pydc detection, while the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) emission from the NMOF core acts as a non-interfering internal calibration.
Lu and Hupp52 (and also Allendorf et al.6 in their review paper) pointed out that one of the major challenges of chemical sensing by MOFs is signal transduction: the cavities of MOFs are often too small to tailor with reporter molecules, i.e. moieties that can readily signal analyte-binding events via changes in colour, redox potential, or other properties. So far, in MOFs, advantage has been taken mainly of the framework luminescence, with signal transduction consisting of luminescence quenching or enhancing (see above). These authors suggested an alternative approach that circumvents the need for molecular-level reporters, relying instead on a readout of changes in a macroscopic property of the sensing material: the refractive index.52 They prepared transparent thin films of zeolitic Zn-imidazolate framework ZIF-8 on an appropriate support material (glass or silicon) and optically monitored the energies of Fabry–Pérot interference peaks as a function of exposure to the analyte. These peaks are observable when the thickness of a supported material is comparable to the wavelength of light. Importantly, their energies also depend on the refractive index of the film. We argue that it may be of interest for a number of applications to combine signal transduction based on fluorescence quenching and refractive index change. This will require the preparation of thin films of LnMOFs.
In most examples presented above the sensing function is based on changes in the intensity of a particular f–f transition. Although often mentioned in the literature, the comparison of the emission of different samples based on the detected intensity may lead to erroneous conclusions (even if all the experimental conditions, such as similar density of emitting centres, excitation wavelength, light source power, excitation/ emission slits, are kept constant during the measurements). Indeed, for instance, the absorption cross-section (and consequently the effective optical path) may change from sample to sample. Therefore, quantum yield, radiance, or quantum efficiency estimates are required to support the comparison of the emission performances of different samples.
In the majority of the examples aforementioned, the sensing of cations, anions or molecules was performed by comparing the intensity of the hypersensitive \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0} \rightarrow {}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2} \mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) transition of one MOF sample exposed to different solvents or gases. The intensity of this transition accounted for both the forced electric dipole and dynamic coupling mechanisms, is strongly dependent on changes in the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) local environment and is dominated by the \(\Omega_{2}\) intensity parameter.53 Although the latter is extremely sensitive to small distortions in the first \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) coordination sphere (essentially on the angular part of the ligand distribution), symmetry alone can not account for the enormous intensity variation sometimes observed. Theory shows that the dynamic coupling contribution is able to account for this vast intensity variation through the polarizabilities of the surrounding atoms, or groups of atoms.53
Therefore, the analysis of the intensity changes must take into account not only possible changes in the first \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) coordination sphere but also modifications in the polarizability of the surrounding ligands.
More insight into the mechanisms behind the variations in the intensity of the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\,\rightarrow\,{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}\) transition may be obtained by measuring the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) emission spectrum from 570 to \(720\ \mathrm{nm}\) \(\mathrm{~\bar{~}^{5}D_{0}~}\rightarrow\mathrm{~\bar{~}^{7}F_{0-4}~}\) transitions). For instance, it is possible to distinguish changes in the first coordination shell (angular and/or radial components) from the increase of the nonradiative de-excitation processes ascribed to the vibrations of organic groups, such as \(\mathrm{C-H}\) , \(\mathrm{O-H}\) , or \(\mathrm{N-H}\) , by analysing the ratio between the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\,\rightarrow\,{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}\) and the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\,\rightarrow\,{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{1}\) transitions \(\left(I_{0-2}/I_{0-1}\right)\) . This latter transition displays a pure magnetic dipole character, not depending on the local ligand field seen by the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions (an internal reference for the entire spectrum). Therefore, if the decrease in the intensity of the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\,\rightarrow\,{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}\) transition is induced by the increase in the nonradiative de-excitation processes of the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\) level, ascribed to the vibrations of organic groups, the \(I_{0-2}/I_{0-1}\) ratio should remain unchanged (as the initial level of the transition is the same). In contrast, alterations in the first coordination sphere (angular and/or radial parts) must necessarily change the \(I_{0-2}/I_{0-1}\) ratio. Moreover, this modification in the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) first coordination sphere can also be discerned in the energy and width of the non-degenerated \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\,\rightarrow\,{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{0}\) line.
## 6. Dual magnetic-photoluminescent systems
In the last few years there has been a clear interest in the preparation of multifunctional materials combining microporosity, optical and magnetic properties. An important object in this field is the synthesis of molecular compounds, in which the magnetic properties are switched by some external perturbation. Such tuneable compounds can be used for future molecular memory and switching devices.54 For example, the structure and properties of photo-controllable molecule based magnetic materials, in particular cyanide-bridged complexes, such as Prussian blue analogues, are well studied.54,55 The structures of these materials (particularly the hybrid ones) and MOFs are similar and, thus, their study may provide insight on how to design materials exhibiting optomagnetic and magneto-optic switching. A case in point is the work of Li et al. showing that the cyanide-bridged heterobimetallic complex \([\mathrm{Nd}(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CN})\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{5}]\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) undergoes a large increase (ca. \(45\%\) ) in magnetic susceptibility upon UV irradiation, in the temperature range of \(5{-}50\ \mathrm{K}\) .56
Ideally, one would wish to tune magnetism with light excitation and light emission with a magnetic field, and realise this with a porous material (e.g., for sensing purposes). Hints that this is possible, comes from the work of Veciana and collaborators.57 They have shown that large-pore \([\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{ptmtc})_{2}(\mathrm{py})_{6}(\mathrm{EtOH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\) exhibits reversible solvent-induced structural changes, which also involve alterations in the magnetic properties. Thus, guest molecules can be used to control the magnetic properties of the compound.
The few LnMOFs systems reported in the literature and highlighted in this section do not represent real opto-magnetic and magneto-optic switches. They are considered 'dual' only
in the sense that the same material displays light emission and a relevant magnetic property, usually a small ferromagnetic coupling between metal ions (here we do not consider trivial systems exhibiting antiferromagnetic coupling).
The general approach used to prepare such dual MOFs has been based on heterometallic frameworks, encompassing \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions and transition metals, that is, d–f systems. In practice, more attention has been devoted to 3d–4f than to 4d–4f systems. The MOF frameworks are usually assembled by mixing the d–f metal ions with polydentate ligands containing O and N donors (such as pyrazinecarboxylic acid, pyridinecarboxylic acid, imidazolecarboxylic acid, ref. 58 and refs. therein) because \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) have higher affinity for hard donors (O), while transition metals favour coordination to soft donors (N). Sometimes, a second ligand is added. For instance, Li et al. chose \(1H\)-benzimidazole-5,6-dicarboxylate (containing up to \(2\,\mathrm{N}\) and \(4\,\mathrm{O}\) donor atoms) to link \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\), and a second ligand (oxalate) to bridge between adjacent \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions.58
Although the study of \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}{-}\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) solids has been popular, recent work focuses on other 3d–4f systems such as \([\mathrm{Dy}(\mathrm{hpd})_{3}\mathrm{Mn}_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]\cdot8.25\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) .59 This material has a honeycomb-type 2D structure with a cavity of ca. \(14.4\mathrm{~\AA~}\) in diameter and displays ferromagnetic coupling between \(\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) . Indeed, the \(\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T\) value increases with decreasing temperature \((20.69\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{K\mol}^{-1}\) at room temperature, only slightly lower than the theoretical value of \(20.74\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\;\mathrm{K}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) expected for 1.5 isolated \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) ions and one \(\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}\) ion in the \({}^{6}\mathrm{F}_{15/2}\) ground state, \(g\,=\,4/3\) ), until it peaks at \(25.22\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{K}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) at \(56\;\mathrm{K}\) , after which it drops considerably due to the decrease of the \(\chi_{\mathrm{~M~}}^{\mathrm{Dy}}T\) value for \(\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}\) . On the other hand, it was found that the emission of this material in DMF increases significantly upon addition of \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) (particularly the hypersensitive \({}^{4}\mathrm{F}_{9/2}\,\rightarrow\,{}^{6}\mathrm{H}_{13/2}\) and \({}^{4}\mathrm{F}_{9/2}\,\rightarrow\,{}^{6}\mathrm{H}_{15/2}\) transitions) and may be used for sensing purposes. The increase of the emission was attributed to coordination of the \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) ions to hydroxyl oxygen atoms of the three ligands \(\mathrm{hpd}^{2-}\) which changes the energy level of the excited state of the ligand. Consequently, the energy of these excited states of \(\mathrm{hpd}^{2-}\) matches those of the lanthanide decreasing the non-radiative energy loss originating from thermal vibration of \(\mathrm{O-H}\) groups.
Chelebaeva et al. reported on a 4d–4f system, \([\mathrm{Nb}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{5}[\mathrm{Mo}(\mathrm{CN})_{8}]\), a new cyano-bridged coordination polymer based on luminescent \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) centres and \([\mathrm{Mo}(\mathrm{CN})_{8}]^{5-}\) building blocks. This solid exhibits the typical emission lines ascribed to the intra-4f8 transitions between the first excited state and the ground multiplet \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}\,\rightarrow\,{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{6-2}\).60 The study found evidence for ferromagnetic interactions between \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Mo}^{5+}\) ions inducing the appearance of a magnetic transition below \(2.3\ \mathrm{K}\). Although many examples of one, two, and three-dimensional cyano-bridged molecule-based magnets based on the association of nd–4f ions have long been known, their photoluminescence properties have been overlooked almost completely.
Another example of a dual 4d–4f system was reported by Zhao et al.,61 in an extension of the above-mentioned report using 4-hydroxypyridine-2,6-dicarboxylic acid to dual \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}{-}\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\) frameworks.42 In this work, although materials with many different types of lanthanides were studied,
evidence for a (weak) ferromagnetic \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}{-}\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) interaction could only be found for \(\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}\). Similar results were obtained by Li et al. for the \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}{-}\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\)frameworks quoted above.58
Interesting photoluminescence and magnetic properties are also exhibited by a number of homometallic \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) MOFs. For example, Huang et al. reported weak \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}{-}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ferromagnetic interactions in \([\mathrm{Tb}_{2}(\mathrm{tdc})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]\) and typical luminescence in the visible region.62 Ferromagnetic \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}{-}\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) coupling was also reported by Harbuzaru et al. for a photoluminescent MOF.47 Weng et al. reported \([\mathrm{Er}(\mathrm{pza})(\mathrm{OH})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\) which showed a ferromagnetic coupling between \(\mathrm{Er}^{3+}\) centres.63
Li et al. described the coordination polymer \([\mathrm{Dy}(\mathrm{cit})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\) exhibiting a 2D layer structure, in which a \(\mathrm{Dy}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) dimer acts as the building block. Weak antiferromagnetic interactions were found between the \(\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}\) ions in the layer.64 With an applied dc magnetic field, unusual magnetic (slow) relaxation was observed in ac susceptibility measurements, which was attributed to the geometric frustration of the structure consisting of an alternate array of square and triangular grids.
## 7. Chiral systems
Chiral coordination polymers are of much interest because they may find applications in a multiplicity of specific areas such as enantioselective synthesis and separation, asymmetric catalysis, magnetism and non-linear optics.65 As far as the latter area is concerned, Allendorf et al. reviewed very briefly work on some emissive MOFs.6 Here we highlight the importance of chiral MOFs displaying helices as an outstanding feature while combining it with porosity, emission and, sometimes, magnetic properties. In general, chiral compounds are prepared either by stereoselective synthesis using enantiopure chiral species (reactants, solvents, templates), or by
spontaneous resolution upon crystallisation using achiral ligands.65 The latter possibility raises two questions: how to (i) generate a chiral SBU from achiral components; and (ii) interlink the resultant chiral SBU into a homochiral structure of higher dimensionality.
Among others, Yuan et al. have recently shown that not only a helix is one of the most outstanding expressions of chirality but it is also a good model to transmit chiral information into MOF systems.65 These authors reported \([\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{tda})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]{\cdot}2n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), a chiral framework based on the linkage of chiral columns constructed from interweaving two different types of helical chains: a right-handed double helix, itself composed of two identical single helical chains formed by \(\mathrm{tda}^{3-}\) bridging adjacent \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\), and a left-handed single helix, formed by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) bridges between \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) centres. A third type of helix is present, constructed from carboxyl groups of \(\mathrm{tda}^{3-}\) bridging \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\). The \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\)-based material was shown to be photoluminescent while the \(\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}\) structure exhibited weak antiferromagnetic coupling between the lanthanide ions.
(S)-Proline and its derivatives show high enantioselectivity in catalysis and are able to produce good yields in many specific reactions. Yue et al. used an enantiomerically pure derivative of (S)-proline, \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{pmp}\), as a chiral building unit and synthesised a series of homochiral porous lanthanide phosphonates formulated as \([\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{pmp})_{3}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\).66 These materials possess a supramolecular framework built up from 1D triple-strand helical chains (Fig. 7a), which consist of phosphonate groups bridging adjacent \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions. The helical chains are stacked through hydrogen bonds to form 1D tubular channels along the \(c\) axis (Fig. 7c). Helical water chains reside within these channels (Fig. 7b), and upon their removal the compounds exhibit selective adsorption capacities for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), and \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\) molecules. The evidence suggests that the helical host acts as a template for the formation of the helical chains of water molecules. The 1D chain arrangements of water molecules are of interest because of their occurrence in several biological processes related to water and ion transport. In this sense, this MOF is a biomimetic system.
The following two examples highlight MOFs that are not chiral (their space groups have a centre of symmetry) although they are built up of helical components. Sun et al. reported a rather complex porous LnMOF system formulated as \([\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(\mathrm{idc})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]{\cdot}1.625\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), possessing helical tubes constructed from interweaving of triple-helical and double-helical chains.67 Two types of helical tubular channels with opposite chirality and achiral channels are present. The \(\mathrm{idc}^{3-}\) ligand is able to effectively sensitise the emission of the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Dy}^{3+}\) compounds, and the latter material displays ferromagnetic coupling between the lanthanides centres. Sun et al. provided an intriguing example of heterometallic \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}{-}\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) organic frameworks, \([\mathrm{LnCd}(\mathrm{idc})(\mathrm{SO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), in which the \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) centres are linked via \(\mathrm{idc}^{3-}\) ligands with skew coordination orientation and featuring left-/right-handed helical tubes (L1/R1) and channels (L2/R2) along the \(b\) axis.68 For the (Tb–Cd), (Eu–Cd), (Dy–Cd) MOFs, efficient energy transfer from \(\mathrm{idc}^{3-}\) to the \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions results in intense green, red and yellow emission, respectively. In contrast, the (Gd–Cd), (Er–Cd), (Yb–Cd) and (Y–Cd) MOFs all display broad
bands, which indicates in turn an energy transfer between \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{idc}^{3-}\).
Jiang et al. have recently presented the first series of enantiomerically pure chiral 3D LnMOFs, \([\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{btc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\cdot1.1(\mathrm{DMF})\) \((\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}=\mathrm{Y}^{3+},\ \mathrm{Tb}^{3+},\ \mathrm{Dy}^{3+},\ \mathrm{Er}^{3+},\ \mathrm{Yb}^{3+})\), which are constructed from achiral ligands under spontaneous resolution without any chiral auxiliary.69 These materials are highly thermostable and permanently porous with high surface areas and they show high potentials for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage. Although no photoluminescence studies were reported the novelty of these MOFs justifies the inclusion of this reference.
## 8. Catalytic systems
The use of LnMOF systems and coordination polymers in catalysis is a poorly explored theme, which clearly deserves more attention. Gándara et al. reported three multifunctional families of materials which were prepared on the basis of different kinds of rare-earth coordination polyhedra.70 The different geometry of derivatives of naphthalenedisulfonic acids \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{nds}\) used as linkers (the 1,5- or the 2,6-disubstituted forms) were on the basis of these three new structural types. These materials, with high thermal stability, act as
active and selective bifunctional heterogeneous catalysts in the oxidation of linalool yielding cyclic hydroxy ethers. Their activity depends more on the number of active centres, with the possibility of increasing the coordination number during the catalytic reaction, than on other structural effects or, for the same structure type, on the rare-earth size. The small size of the pore in these structures avoids the accessibility of the substrate to the metallic centres; thus the catalytic reactions take place on the surface of the crystallites.
Cunha-Silva et al. prepared a series of layered networks formulated as \([\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cmp})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\).71 These materials were tested in the cyclodehydration of D-xylose to furfural, and the results revealed a performance similar to that of other solid-acid catalysts used in the same reaction under similar conditions. A detailed catalytic study was performed for the \(\mathrm{Y}^{3+}\)-based compound: selectivity increased to \(84\%\) as the conversion reached \(83\%\). This solid was also re-used successfully in three consecutive \(4\ \mathrm{h}\) runs after separation from the liquid phase by centrifugation and regeneration. The photoluminescence properties of the materials were studied in detail. In particular, it was shown that the mixed lanthanide diluted \(\left[(\mathrm{Gd}_{0.95}\mathrm{Eu}_{0.05})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cmp})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right]\) material displays \(\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}\)-to-\(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) energy transfer within the layers.
Amghouz et al. reported chiral heterometallic MOFs, assembled from \(\mathbf{Y}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) and flexible-achiral rigid dicarboxylate ligands. The materials formulated as \([\mathrm{NaY}(\mathrm{tart})(\mathrm{bpdc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\) and \([\mathrm{NaY}(\mathrm{tart})(\mathrm{bdc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\), were obtained as single crystalline phases under hydrothermal conditions.72 The materials were assessed as Lewis acid catalysts in the acetylation of benzaldehyde. However, the catalytic activity was found to be quite modest.
Choi et al., discussed a very interesting example of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\)-based NMOF about \(200\,\mathrm{nm}\) in length and \(50\,\mathrm{nm}\) in diameter, a BET surface area of \(1001\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), pore volume of \(0.50\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and pore diameter of \(2\,\mathrm{nm}\).73 The particles work as a size-selective photocatalyst for the one-electron oxidation of organic compounds. The electron transfer between various organic compounds, such as aromatic sulfides and amines adsorbed on the surface and trapped inside the pores of the NMOF structure, to photoexcited nanoparticles was addressed by time-resolved emission measurements. Confocal fluorescence microscopy of the microsized \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\)-based MOF was used to examine how deeply quenchers penetrate the porous MOF structure, investigating the change in fluorescent images before and after the addition of electron donors with different molecular sizes. Spatially resolved emission quenching images obtained by confocal fluorescence microscopy revealed that small (large) quencher molecules quickly (slowly) and homogeneously (inhomogeneously) penetrate microsized Eu-MOF crystals (Fig. 8).
Other innovative reports of LnMOFs catalytic systems which, however, do not present photoluminescence studies were presented by Gustafsson et al.74 and Vitorino et al.75
## 9. Multimodal imaging systems
Only a handful of examples are available on the use of LnMOFs as potential multimodal, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)76 and optical contrast enhancing agents. To the best of our knowledge, the first work was reported by the group of Lin and describes the reverse microemulsion synthesis of LnMOF nanorods of \([\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{bdc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\) and nanoplates of \([\mathrm{Gd}(1,2,4\mathrm{-btc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). These materials display large longitudinal and transverse relaxivities (\(R_{1}\) and \(R_{2}\), respectively), on a per \(\mathrm{mM}\) of \(\mathrm{Gd}^{3+}\) basis, and exceedingly large \(R_{1}\) and \(R_{2}\) values on a per \(\mathrm{mM}\) of nanoparticles basis. Because \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\)- and \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\)-doped \([\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{bdc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]\) nanorods were also prepared and proved to be photoluminescent, these particles have potential for being used as effective contrast agents in multimodal imaging. In a subsequent work, the same group used a surfactant-assisted method at elevated temperatures to prepare nanoparticles of \([\mathrm{Gd}_{2}(\mathrm{bhc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]\) and \([\mathrm{Gd}_{2}(\mathrm{bhc})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{8}](\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\) and also \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\)- and \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\)-doped materials and, again, proved their potential in bimodal imaging.
Pereira et al. studied the \([\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{cmp})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\) system reported by Cunha-Silva et al. (see previous subsection),71 and found that microparticles are very efficient as potential MRI contrast agents for \(T_{2}\)-weighted imaging, but significantly less promising for \(T_{1}\)-weighted imaging, despite the presence of one water molecule in the inner-sphere of the lanthanide coordination environment.77 This is the first work on MOFs that examines in detail the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) relaxivity mechanisms.
Zhu et al. reported interesting frameworks based on macrocyclic polyamines. The compounds have been used in the last two decades or so as chelating ligands with \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) centres for the preparation of MRI contrast agents. However, only the photoluminescence properties were reported.78 Clearly, it would be interesting to compare the performance as MRI contrast agents of the LnMOFs \([\mathrm{Ln}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{teta})]\mathrm{NO}_{3}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) with that of the isolated complexes in solution.
## 10. Conclusions and outlook
The remarkable structural diversity, combining organic and inorganic components and porosity, afford LnMOFs real potential for applications in light-emitting devices and sensors. The field is, however, in its infancy and much remains to be investigated before photonic prototypes (e.g. tunable lasers, LEDs, optical amplifiers or light conversion molecular devices) become a commercial reality. We now summarise some of the research themes which deserve particular attention.
(a) More research is needed on the preparation and functionalisation of LnMOFs nanoparticles because many practical applications will require them. For example, NMOFs contrast agents may have superior relaxivity to small-molecule contrast agents, while also providing effective optical contrast.76
(b) As mentioned before, LnMOFs are still at an early stage of development. The papers published so far have been essentially devoted to the photoluminescence of green \((\mathrm{Tb}^{3+})\) and red \((\mathrm{Eu}^{3+})\) light emitters, using UV or NIR excitation (down and up-conversion, respectively). Materials acting as efficient blue emitters are still scarce, despite the intriguing examples of visible-to-visible UC blue emitting \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\)-MOFs. The examples reporting NIR luminescence are often poorly characterised, lacking absorption and excitation spectra, radiative and non-radiative lifetimes, intensity parameters and quantum efficiency. Also little work is available on the possibility of extending the excitation range to the long UV and blue spectral regions (using, for instance, cheap, safe and cold LEDs). Bimodal or multicolored emission, achieved by combining \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) ions emitting in different spectral regions, remains largely unexplored, despite its potential for being used in innovative barcodes and in white-light phosphors. Few papers study the latter issue, which has been addressed, e.g., via the superposition of the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{6-3}\mathrm{~Tb}^{3+}\) or \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{0-4}\mathrm{~Eu}^{3+}\) transitions with, respectively, a ligand-to-metal charge-transfer band or \(\mathrm{V}^{4+}\) emission.20,79 Only a small number of cases address the real design of the ligands for target light-emitting functions (understanding and optimising the ligand-to-emitting centers energy-transfer processes).
(c) Porous LnMOFs present clear potential for sensing molecules, cations and anions. The mechanisms by which the LnMOFs exhibit a change in the photoluminescence response to an interaction with the analyte remain, however, by and large, a matter of educated guess and speculation. In most cases, the sensing function is based on changes in the intensity of a particular f–f transition, not on a frequency shift. The comparison of the emission of different samples based on the detected intensity may lead to erroneous conclusions;
quantum yields and radiance or quantum efficiency must be measured. The research still focuses on the development of LnMOF materials and characterisation of their properties and essentially no real sensing devices are available.
(d) One of the most attractive features of LnMOFs is their multifunctionality, in particular the possibility of combining in a solid microporosity and optical and magnetic properties. So far, the reported systems are dual only in the sense that the same material displays light emission and a relevant magnetic property. Although real MOFs exhibiting magneto-optic and opto-magnetic switching probably have not been reported, much work is available on related compounds, in particular cyanide-bridged complexes, such as Prussian blue analogues. The lessons learnt with the latter may inspire the design of the former.
(e) The conceptual and practical importance of chiral LnMOFs in enantioselective synthesis and separation, asymmetric catalysis, magnetism and non-linear optics should be taken into due consideration.
(f) Catalysis by LnMOFs deserves much more attention. The intriguing possibility of following the reaction progress by monitoring changes in luminescence may open up new areas of research.
In the beginning of this critical review we set out the grand challenges in materials engineering of luminescent centres. What can we say about the real contribution that the work on LnMOFs has made, so far, to meet these challenges? Perhaps the most important conclusion is that, in general, the research needs to address these issues using an integrated approach. For example, when designing a LnMOF for sensing applications, one needs to consider (i) antennae ligands (perhaps fluorinated) that afford high brightness and emission quantum yield and broadened excitation range; (ii) the chosen ligands must be photostable under irradiation and they must also be (iii) environmentally-friendly; (iv) all this should be combined with, e.g., porosity. Most research has concentrated on (i) and (iv). Surprisingly, the vast majority of MOFs described to date are composed of organic ligands derived from non-renewable feedstocks (see, e.g., a recent paper on MOFs from edible sources),80 while the LnMOFs photostability is often overlooked. Finally, to the best of our knowledge, no electroluminescent materials are available based on \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) emitters as MOF-nodes and electron conducting linkers, which would permit tuning the wavelength of light emission. This would require processing the materials, for example as films.81
In summary, there is plenty of room for bottom-up engineering of luminescent centres in LnMOF materials.
